%% Compensation: payee account does not exist, so the debit is reversed.
%% Source: ARCHITECTURE.md section 11, "Walkthrough: payee account does not exist",
%% plus the Resolve fence before the failure branch (ADR-024).
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor C as Client
    participant G as Gateway
    participant O as Orchestrator
    participant A as Mock Bank A
    participant L as Ledger
    participant B as Mock Bank B

    C->>G: POST /v1/transfers (payee ACC999 does not exist)
    G->>O: CreateTransfer

    Note over O: INITIATED -> DEBIT_SENT, then the debit succeeds.
    O->>A: Debit(reference_id, amount)
    A-->>O: APPLIED
    O->>L: PostJournal(debit-leg)<br/>D receivable:bank_a / C transit
    L-->>O: ok
    Note over O: state = DEBITED -> CREDIT_SENT

    O->>B: Credit(reference_id, amount)
    B-->>O: NOT_FOUND<br/>x-error-code: ACCOUNT_NOT_FOUND
    Note over O: classify() = DEFINITE_FAILURE.<br/>Fence first (ADR-024): an earlier Credit attempt<br/>could still be on its way and apply later.
    O->>B: Resolve(reference_id, CREDIT)
    Note over B: Inserts a VOIDED tombstone.<br/>A late Credit now fails REFERENCE_VOIDED.
    B-->>O: VOIDED
    Note over O: Credit failing definitely means REVERSING,<br/>never FAILED - the payer has already been debited.
    O->>O: CAS to REVERSING<br/>failure_code = PAYEE_ACCOUNT_NOT_FOUND

    O->>A: ReverseDebit(reference_id)
    Note over A: Reads the APPLIED DEBIT row, inserts<br/>REVERSE_DEBIT, adds the amount back.<br/>Succeeds even if the account is now BLOCKED -<br/>a refund must always succeed.
    A-->>O: APPLIED, balance_after
    O->>L: PostJournal(transfer:{id}:reversal)<br/>D transit / C receivable:bank_a
    L-->>O: ok
    Note over L: The reversal undoes the debit leg exactly.<br/>transit returns to zero.
    O->>O: CAS to REVERSED (terminal)

    O-->>G: Transfer{state=REVERSED,<br/>failure_code=PAYEE_ACCOUNT_NOT_FOUND}
    G-->>C: 422 Unprocessable Entity, status REVERSED

    Note over C,B: Business failures are gRPC OK with a failure_code,<br/>not gRPC errors. Money is conserved: the payer<br/>was debited and then refunded.
